=begin pod

=NAME
Selkie::UI

=TITLE
UI Builder Abstractions for Selkie

=NOTE
This module is under active development. API may change.

=SYNOPSIS

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Selkie::UI;

App {
    Screen :name<main>, {
        VBox {
            Button.label: "Click me"
        }
    }
}
=end code

=DESCRIPTION

Selkie::UI provides a Raku-native DSL for building terminal user interfaces using the
L<Selkie|https://raku.land/zef:apogee/Selkie> framework. Unlike traditional imperative
UI code where you manually manage rendering and event loops, this module offers a
declarative approach — your code describes B<what> the UI should look like and do,
not B<how> to achieve it step-by-step.

Widgets are declared hierarchically using block syntax, with properties and handlers
specified via chained method calls. This makes UI definitions intuitive, compositional,
and easy to reason about — whether you are building simple tools or complex applications.

The DSL handles the glue between your declarative definitions and Selkie's imperative
runtime, including state management with automatic UI updates when reactive variables
change.

=head1 Dynamic Variables

Builder context flows through dynamic variables. These are set by C<App>, C<Screen>,
and layout containers, and are only available within their blocks:

=item1 C<$*UI-APP> — The running application object (store, rendering, event loop)
=item1 C<$*UI-PARENT> — The parent builder for tree hierarchy
=item1 C<@*UI-PATHS> — Tracks state paths read during block evaluation (for auto-subscribe)
=item1 C<@*UI-NODES> — Stack of child nodes being constructed
=item1 C<$*UI-ID> — Unique string per auto-subscribe chain for event deduplication

=head1 State Management

=head2 new-state

C<new-state($default, :$name, :$event)> creates a reactive scalar state variable
that automatically dispatches updates and re-renders affected UI elements. Returns
a Proxy where reads track the state path in C<@*UI-PATHS> and writes dispatch
events through the store. Use for scalar values (Str, Int, Bool).

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $counter := new-state 0;
$counter = 42;
=end code

=head2 new-array-state

C<new-array-state(@default, :$name, :$event)> returns a L<ReactiveArray> object
that implements C<Positional> and C<Iterable>. Bind with C<:=> to an C<@>-sigiled
variable. Mutation methods (C<ASSIGN-POS>, C<push>, C<pop>, C<shift>, C<unshift>,
C<splice>) dispatch fresh values to the store.

For nested mutations, use the extract-modify-reassign pattern:

=begin code :lang<raku>
my @tasks := new-array-state [{:title<A>,:!done},{:title<B>,:!done}];
my %task = @tasks[0];
%task<done> = True;
@tasks[0] = %task;   # ASSIGN-POS dispatches fresh Array
=end code

=head2 new-hash-state

C<new-hash-state(%default, :$name, :$event)> returns a L<ReactiveHash> object
that implements C<Associative> and C<Iterable>. Bind with C<:=> to a C<%>-sigiled
variable. Mutation methods (C<ASSIGN-KEY>, C<DELETE-KEY>) dispatch fresh values
to the store.

=begin code :lang<raku>
my %config := new-hash-state {:theme<dark>, :refresh(30)};
%config<theme> = 'light';   # ASSIGN-KEY dispatches fresh Hash
=end code

=head1 Block-Based Setters

Many builder methods accept a block instead of a literal value. When a block is
provided, the builder:

=item1 Evaluates the block once to get the initial value
=item1 Tracks any C<new-state> variables read inside the block via C<@*UI-PATHS>
=item1 Subscribes to those state paths so the setter re-runs when states change
=item1 Updates the widget with the new value automatically

Example:

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $counter := new-state 0;
Text.text: { "Count: $counter" };
=end code

These reactive setters require an active C<App> because auto-subscribe uses
C<$*UI-APP> and the store.

=head1 Builder Lifecycle

=item1 C<TWEAK> fires on construction, pushing the builder onto C<@*UI-NODES> for parent capture
=item1 Parent iterates C<@*UI-NODES> after the block to collect children
=item1 Chained method calls configure the underlying C<.obj> widget
=item1 Block-valued setters trigger auto-subscribe for reactive updates

When building outside an C<App> (e.g., in tests), use C<Detached> to set up
C<@*UI-NODES>:

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $stream = Detached { TextStream };
=end code

=head1 Builders

The module exports builder functions for Selkie primitives. Each builder returns a
chainable builder object that wraps the underlying widget.

=head2 Layout and Containers

=item1 C<App> — Application entry point that initializes the UI and starts the event loop
=item1 C<Screen> — Named screens that can be switched between
=item1 C<Tab> — Named screen for use inside C<TabBar> (creates a Screen)
=item1 C<VBox>, C<HBox>, C<Split> — Layout containers for stacking or splitting widgets
=item1 C<Border>, C<Modal>, C<ScrollView> — Container and decorator widgets
=item1 C<CardList> — Scrollable list of variable-height widgets

=head2 Inputs and Selection

=item1 C<Button> — Clickable button with label and press handler
=item1 C<TextInput>, C<MultiLineInput> — Text input fields with submit handling
=item1 C<Checkbox>, C<RadioGroup>, C<Select> — Selection controls
=item1 C<ProgressBar> — Progress indicator

=head2 Text and Lists

=item1 C<Text>, C<TextStream>, C<RichText> — Text display widgets
=item1 C<ListView>, C<Table>, C<TabBar> — List and table widgets
=item1 C<Image>, C<Spinner>, C<Toast> — Media and status widgets

=head2 Overlays and Helpers

=item1 C<ConfirmModal> — Confirmation modal builder
=item1 C<CommandPalette> — Fuzzy command launcher
=item1 C<FileBrowser> — File picker modal
=item1 C<HelpOverlay> — Contextual keybind help
=item1 C<PasswordStrength> — Password strength meter

=head2 Charts

=item1 C<Plot>, C<BarChart>, C<LineChart>, C<ScatterPlot> — Chart widgets
=item1 C<Sparkline>, C<Heatmap>, C<Histogram> — Inline and grid charts
=item1 C<Axis>, C<Legend> — Chart adornments

=head1 Helpers

App helpers that interact with the runtime:

=item1 C<Handler> — Register a store event handler
=item1 C<Detached> — Create isolated UI nodes without parent attachment
=item1 C<OnKey> — Register global key handlers
=item1 C<OnFrame> — Register a per-frame callback
=item1 C<Dispatch> — Dispatch events to the store
=item1 C<Tick> — Tick the store immediately
=item1 C<ShowModal> — Open a modal dialog
=item1 C<CloseModal> — Close the currently open modal
=item1 C<Focus> — Focus a specific widget (returns the widget for chaining)
=item1 C<FocusNext> — Move focus to the next widget
=item1 C<FocusPrevious> — Move focus to the previous widget
=item1 C<SwitchScreen> — Switch to a named screen
=item1 C<Quit> — Quit the application
=item1 C<Toast> — Show a transient toast message

=head1 Playground

There is a built-in playground to live-edit/test Selkie::UI code:

=begin code
raku -I lib bin/selkie-ui-playground.raku
=end code

=para L<![](./playground.gif)|./playground.gif>

=head1 Examples

=head2 Text input with reactive display

This example creates a text input field that echoes user input to a text stream above it.

The program creates a reactive string variable with C<new-state>, places a vertical box
layout on screen, adds a text stream widget to display messages, and adds a text input
widget. When the user types and presses Enter, the input text is stored in the reactive
variable, which automatically updates the text stream, and the input field is cleared
for the next entry.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Selkie::UI;

App {
    my $next-msg := new-state Str;
    VBox {
        TextStream.append: { $next-msg };
        TextInput(:placeholder('Type here...')).size(1).on-submit: -> $input, $text {
            $next-msg = $text;
            $input.clear
        }
    }
}
=end code

The C<App> block is the entry point that initializes the application. The C<new-state>
function creates a reactive state variable bound with C<:=>, initialized as an empty
string. The C<VBox> widget arranges its children vertically from top to bottom.
C<TextStream.append> with a block argument reactively displays the value of C<$next-msg>
and updates whenever it changes. C<TextInput> creates a single-line text input with a
placeholder hint. C<.size(1)> constrains the input to a fixed height of one row.
C<.on-submit> registers a handler that runs when the user presses Enter. The handler
receives the input widget and the submitted text, stores the text in the reactive
variable, and clears the input for the next entry.

=para L<![](./text.gif)|./text.gif>

=head2 Button with reactive state

This example shows a button whose label changes based on a numeric state variable.

The program creates a reactive integer counter, places it in a vertical box, and displays
a button. The button label is computed from the counter value using a block — when the
counter changes, the label automatically re-renders. Each button press increments the
counter, updating both the counter value and the button label.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Selkie::UI;

App {
    my UInt $val := new-state 0;
    VBox {
        Button.label({ $val ?? "BLE $val" !! "BLA $val" })
            .on-press: { ++$val }
    }
}
=end code

=para L<![](./test.gif)|./test.gif>

=head2 ListView with reactive items

This example binds a list view to a reactive array. When the array changes, the list
updates automatically.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Selkie::UI;

App {
    my $items := new-state <One Two Three>;
    VBox {
        ListView.set-items: { $items };
        Button.label('Refresh').on-press: {
            $items = <One Two Three Four>
        }
    }
}
=end code

=head2 TabBar with tabs

This example shows a TabBar with two tabs, each containing different content.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Selkie::UI;

App {
    Screen :name<main>, {
        TabBar {
            Tab { Text(:text('Dashboard')) }: :name<dash>, :label('Dashboard');
            Tab { Text(:text('Settings')) }:  :name<set>,  :label('Settings');
            Border   # content area — TabBar fills it with the active tab
        }
    }
}
=end code

=head1 EXPORTS

=head2 App and Screen

=for head2 :numbered
App(&block, |c)

Application entry point that initializes the UI and starts the event loop.

The block receives the builder as C<$_> and runs inside the application context.
All builder functions must be called inside an C<App> block.

=begin code :lang<raku>
App {
    Screen :name<main>, { VBox { Button.label: 'Hello' } }
}
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Screen(&block, Str :$name = "main", |c)

Named screens that can be switched between with C<SwitchScreen>.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Screen :name<settings>, { VBox { Text(:text('Settings')) } }
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Screen($node, Str :$name = "main", |c)

Screen variant that accepts an existing widget node instead of a block.

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $node = Detached { VBox { Button.label: 'Ok' } };
Screen($node): :name<main>;
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Tab(&block, Str :$name!, Str :$label!, |c)

Named screen for use inside a C<TabBar>. Returns a ScreenBuilder. The block
receives the screen builder as C<$_>.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Tab { Text(:text('Dashboard')) }: :name<dash>, :label('Dashboard');
=end code

=head2 Layout Containers

=for head2 :numbered
VBox(&block, :$size, :$style, |c)

Vertical box layout. Stacks children top-to-bottom.

=begin code :lang<raku>
VBox {
    Button.label: 'Top';
    Button.label: 'Bottom'
}
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
HBox(&block, :$size, :$style, |c)

Horizontal box layout. Stacks children left-to-right.

=begin code :lang<raku>
HBox {
    Button.label: 'Left';
    Button.label: 'Right'
}
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Split(&block?, :$ratio, :$size, :$style, |c)

Split container. Divides space between children. Optional C<:$ratio> sets
the split proportion.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Split :ratio(0.3), {
    Text(:text('Left'));
    Text(:text('Right'))
}
=end code

=head2 Inputs and Selection

=for head2 :numbered
Button(:$label, :$size, :$style, |c)

Pressable button with label. Supports C<.on-press> handler (idempotent).

=begin code :lang<raku>
Button.label('Click').on-press: { say 'clicked' }
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.label(Str)> / C<.label(&block)> — Set or reactively bind label text
=item1 C<.on-press(&block)> — Register click handler (idempotent)

=for head2 :numbered
TextInput(:$placeholder, :$size, :$style, |c)

Single-line text input field.

=begin code :lang<raku>
TextInput(:placeholder('Name')).on-submit: -> $_, $text { say $text }
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.text(Str)> / C<.text(&block)> — Get/set text
=item1 C<.text-silent(Str)> — Set text without triggering events
=item1 C<.on-submit(&block)> — Submit handler (idempotent)
=item1 C<.on-change(&block)> — Change handler (idempotent)
=item1 C<.clear> / C<.clear(&block)> — Clear input
=item1 C<.mask(:$char!)> — Set mask character for password input

=for head2 :numbered
MultiLineInput(:$placeholder, :$max-lines, :$size, :$style, |c)

Multi-line text input area.

=begin code :lang<raku>
MultiLineInput(:max-lines(5)).on-submit: -> $_, $text { say $text.lines }
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Checkbox(:$label, :$size, :$style, |c)

Toggleable checkbox with label.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Checkbox(:label('Accept terms')).check(True).on-change: -> $, $checked { say $checked }
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.label(Str)> / C<.label(&block)> — Set or reactively bind label
=item1 C<.check(Bool)> / C<.check(&block)> — Set checked state
=item1 C<.on-change(&block)> — Change handler (idempotent)

=for head2 :numbered
RadioGroup(:$size, :$style, |c)

Group of radio buttons for exclusive selection.

=begin code :lang<raku>
RadioGroup.set-items: ['Option A', 'Option B']
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Select(:$size, :$style, |c)

Dropdown-style selection widget.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Select.set-items: ['option-1', 'Option 2']
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
PasswordStrength(:$input!, :$show-label, :$size, :$style, |c)

Password input with strength meter visualization.

=begin code :lang<raku>
PasswordStrength(:input($input-builder), :show-label)
=end code

=head2 Text and Rich Content

=for head2 :numbered
Text(&block?, :$text, :$size, :$style, |c)

Static text display widget. The optional block variant supports reactive
subscriptions when used inside C<App>.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Text(:text('Hello world'));
Text.text: { "Count: $counter" }  # reactive — reruns when $counter changes
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.text(Str)> / C<.text(&block)> — Set or reactively bind text
=item1 C<.text-silent(Str)> — Set text without triggering events

=for head2 :numbered
TextStream(:$placeholder, :$size, :$style, |c)

Scrollable text stream for streaming log-like content.

=begin code :lang<raku>
TextStream.append: 'log line';
TextStream.append: { $message }  # reactive — appends when $message changes
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.append(Str)> / C<.append(&block)> — Append text (block = reactive)
=item1 C<.clear(Bool)> / C<.clear(&block)> — Clear the stream
=item1 C<.max-lines(UInt)> — Limit visible lines

=for head2 :numbered
RichText(:$truncated-top, :$truncated-bottom, :$size, :$style, |c)

Rich text widget with styled spans.

=begin code :lang<raku>
RichText.content: ['plain', %(:text<styled>, :style{ :sgr<bold> })]
=end code

=head2 Lists and Tables

=for head2 :numbered
ListView(&block?, :$size, :$style, |c)

Scrollable list of items. Supports C<.set-items> for reactive data.

=begin code :lang<raku>
ListView.set-items: ['A', 'B', 'C'];
ListView.set-items: { @items };
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.set-items(@items)> / C<.set-items(&block)> — Set list items
=item1 C<.on-select(&block)> — Selection handler (idempotent)
=item1 C<.on-activate(&block)> — Activation handler (idempotent)
=item1 C<.on-key(Str $key, &block)> — Key handler (idempotent)
=item1 C<.cursor> — Current cursor position

=for head2 :numbered
Table(&block?, :$show-scrollbar, :$size, :$style, |c)

Tabular data display with column headers. Accepts columns as array of hashes
with C<:name>, C<:label>, C<:sizing>/C<:flex>/C<:fixed>, C<:sortable>,
C<:render>, and C<:sort-key>. The optional block sets row data reactively.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Table :columns[
    %(:name<id>,   :label('ID')),
    %(:name<name>, :label('Name')),
], {
    [{:id(1), :name('Alice')}, {:id(2), :name('Bob')}]
}
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.add-column(:$name!, :$label!, ...)> — Add a column
=item1 C<.rows(@rows)> / C<.rows(&block)> — Set or reactively bind rows
=item1 C<.clear-columns> — Remove all columns
=item1 C<.on-select(&block)> — Selection handler (idempotent)
=item1 C<.on-activate(&block)> — Activation handler (idempotent)
=item1 C<.on-key(Str $key, &block)> — Key handler

=for head2 :numbered
CardList(&block?, :$select-first, :$select-last, :$size, :$style, |c)

Scrollable list of variable-height widget cards. Each item is a widget with a
required height. Supports reactive data via block setters.

=begin code :lang<raku>
CardList { $.add-item(Text(:text('card')), :height(3)) }
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.add-item($widget, :$height!, :$root, :$border)> — Add a card
=item1 C<.set-items(@items)> / C<.set-items(&block)> — Replace all items
=item1 C<.clear-items> — Remove all items
=item1 C<.set-item-height(Int $idx, Int $height)> — Change item height
=item1 C<.select-index(Int $idx)> — Select item by index
=item1 C<.select-first(Bool)> / C<.select-last(Bool)> — Select first/last
=item1 C<.scroll-up> / C<.scroll-down> — Scroll by one item
=item1 C<.on-select(&block)> — Selection handler (idempotent)

=for head2 :numbered
TabBar(&block?, :$active-tab, :$size, :$style, |c)

Tabbed navigation bar. Uses a declarative block-based API. The block must
return a content container (e.g., Border) that provides C<.title> and
C<.content> methods. Use C<Tab> inside the block to define screens.

=begin code :lang<raku>
TabBar {
    Tab { Text(:text('Dashboard')) }: :name<dash>, :label('Dashboard');
    Tab { Text(:text('Settings')) }:  :name<set>,  :label('Settings');
    Border   # returned as the content area
}
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.add-tab(:$name!, :$label!)> — Register a tab
=item1 C<.remove-tab(Str $name)> — Remove a tab
=item1 C<.clear-tabs> — Remove all tabs
=item1 C<.select-by-name(Str $name)> — Select tab by name
=item1 C<.select-index(UInt $idx)> — Select tab by index
=item1 C<.on-tab-selected(&block)> — Tab change handler (idempotent)

=head2 Overlays and Helpers

=for head2 :numbered
Border(&block?, :$title, :$hide-top-border, :$hide-bottom-border, :$size, :$style, |c)

Bordered container with optional title. Supports reactive title via block.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Border :title('Section'), { Text(:text('Content')) }
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Modal(Selkie::UI::Base $widget)

Extracts the C<.modal> widget from an existing builder. Useful with C<ShowModal>.

=begin code :lang<raku>
ShowModal(Modal($my-builder));  # $my-builder.modal
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Modal(&block?, :$width-ratio, :$height-ratio, :$dim-background, :$size, :$style, |c)

Modal overlay dialog with configurable size and backdrop dimming.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Modal :width-ratio(0.6), :height-ratio(0.5), { Text(:text('Overlay')) }
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
ConfirmModal(:$title, :$message, :$yes-label, :$no-label, :$width-ratio, :$height-ratio, :on-result, |c)

Confirmation dialog with accept/cancel buttons.

=begin code :lang<raku>
ConfirmModal :title('Confirm'), :message('Proceed?'), :on-result(-> $, $result { say $result })
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Toast(Str $message, Numeric :$duration = 3e0)

Temporary toast notification dispatched via the app.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Toast('Saved!', :duration(2));
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
HelpOverlay(:$app!, :$focused-widget, :$size, :$style, |c)

Keyboard shortcut help overlay.

=begin code :lang<raku>
HelpOverlay   # auto-generates from app keybindings
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
CommandPalette(&block?, :$size, :$style, |c)

Command palette for fuzzy-searchable actions. The optional block
registers commands.

=begin code :lang<raku>
CommandPalette { $.add-command({ say 'new' }, :label<New>) }
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.add-command(&action, :$label!)> — Register a command
=item1 C<.commands(@pairs)> — Set commands from pairs
=item1 C<.show-modal> — Show the palette modal
=item1 C<.on-command(&block)> — Command handler (idempotent)
=item1 C<.build> — Build the underlying modal widget

=for head2 :numbered
FileBrowser(&block?, Bool :$show-modal, Bool :$focus, :&on-select, :$size, :$style, |c)

File system browser widget that opens as a modal.

=begin code :lang<raku>
FileBrowser :show-modal, :on-select(-> $, $path { say $path })
=end code

=head2 Miscellaneous

=for head2 :numbered
ScrollView(&block?, :$show-scrollbar, :$size, :$style, |c)

Scrollable viewport container for overflowing content.

=begin code :lang<raku>
ScrollView { VBox { ... } }
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Spinner(:@frames, :$interval, :$style, :$size, |c)

Animated spinner with configurable frames and interval.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Spinner(:frames(['/', '-', '\\', '|']), :interval(0.1))
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.tick> — Advance to next frame
=item1 C<.reset> — Reset to first frame

=for head2 :numbered
Image(&block?, :$file, :$size, :$style, |c)

Image display widget. Supports reactive file binding via block.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Image(:file('logo.png'))
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
ProgressBar(:$size, :$style, |c)

Progress bar with configurable value.

=begin code :lang<raku>
ProgressBar.progress(0.75).show-percentage
=end code

Methods:
=item1 C<.progress(Numeric)> / C<.progress(&block)> — Set progress value
=item1 C<.indeterminate(Bool)> — Toggle indeterminate mode
=item1 C<.show-percentage(Bool)> — Toggle percentage display
=item1 C<.tick> — Advance animation frame

=head2 Charts

=for head2 :numbered
Plot(:$type, :$min-y, :$max-y, :$title, :$gridtype, :$rangex, :@store-path, :$empty-message, :$size, :$style, |c)

General-purpose plot widget.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Plot :type<bar>, :data([{:x(0),:y(1)},{:x(1),:y(3)}]), :title('Sales')
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
BarChart(:@data, :@store-path, :$orientation, :$palette, :$show-axis, :$show-labels, :$min, :$max, :$tick-count, :$empty-message, :$size, :$style, |c)

Bar chart with configurable orientation and styling.

=begin code :lang<raku>
BarChart(:data([{:label<A>,:value(10)},{:label<B>,:value(20)}]))
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
LineChart(:@series, :&store-path-fn, :$palette, :$show-axis, :$show-legend, :$fill-below, :$overlap, :$y-min, :$y-max, :$tick-count, :$empty-message, :$size, :$style, |c)

Line chart with multiple series support.

=begin code :lang<raku>
LineChart(:series([{:name<A>,:values[1,2,3]}]))
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
ScatterPlot(:@series, :@store-path, :$palette, :$x-min, :$x-max, :$y-min, :$y-max, :$overlap, :$empty-message, :$size, :$style, |c)

Scatter plot for point data visualization.

=begin code :lang<raku>
ScatterPlot(:series([{:name<pts>,:x[1,2,3],:y[4,5,6]}]))
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Sparkline(:@data, :@store-path, :$min, :$max, :$empty-message, :$size, :$style, |c)

Compact inline sparkline chart.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Sparkline(:data([1, 3, 2, 5, 4]))
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Heatmap(:@data, :@store-path, :$ramp, :$min, :$max, :$empty-message, :$size, :$style, |c)

Heatmap for matrix data visualization.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Heatmap(:data([[1,2],[3,4]]))
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Histogram(:@values, :$bins, :@bin-edges, :$orientation, :$palette, :$show-axis, :$show-labels, :$min, :$max, :$tick-count, :$empty-message, :$size, :$style, |c)

Histogram for distribution visualization.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Histogram(:values([1,1,2,2,2,3]), :bins(3))
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Axis(:$min!, :$max!, :$edge, :$tick-count, :$show-line, :$size, :$style, |c)

Chart axis with configurable range and ticks.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Axis(:min(0), :max(100), :tick-count(5))
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
Legend(:@series, :$orientation, :$swatch, :$size, :$style, |c)

Chart legend for multi-series plots.

=begin code :lang<raku>
Legend(:series([{:name<A>,:swatch<red>}]))
=end code

=head2 State and Helpers

=for head2 :numbered
new-state($default, :$name, :$event)

Creates a reactive state variable. Returns a Proxy that auto-tracks reads and dispatches
events on writes. Use for scalar values (Str, Int, Bool). Requires an active C<App> context.

=for head2 :numbered
new-array-state(@default, :$name, :$event)

Creates a reactive array state. Returns a C<ReactiveArray> that implements C<Positional>
and C<Iterable>. Bind with C<:=> to an C<@>-sigiled variable. Mutations dispatch
fresh values: C<push>, C<pop>, C<shift>, C<unshift>, C<splice>, C<ASSIGN-POS>.

=for head2 :numbered
new-hash-state(%default, :$name, :$event)

Creates a reactive hash state. Returns a C<ReactiveHash> that implements C<Associative>
and C<Iterable>. Bind with C<:=> to a C<%>-sigiled variable. Mutations dispatch
fresh values: C<ASSIGN-KEY>, C<DELETE-KEY>.

=for head2 :numbered
Handler(Str $name, &block)

Registers a store event handler for the given event name.

=for head2 :numbered
Detached(&block)

Creates isolated UI node contexts without parent attachment. Sets up C<@*UI-NODES>
for the block. Returns the block's result if defined, otherwise the first child
node from C<@*UI-NODES>.

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $stream = Detached { TextStream };
=end code

=for head2 :numbered
OnKey(Str:D $spec, &handler, Str :$screen)

Registers a keypress handler. C<$spec> is a key specification string
(e.g., C<"ctrl+p">, C<"q">, C<"tab">).

=for head2 :numbered
OnFrame(&block)

Registers a per-frame callback for animation or polling.

=for head2 :numbered
Dispatch($event, *%payload)

Dispatches a named event through the store with optional payload.

=for head2 :numbered
Tick

Triggers an immediate store tick (re-render cycle).

=for head2 :numbered
ShowModal($modal)

Opens a modal dialog from a builder or widget. Returns the modal widget.

=for head2 :numbered
CloseModal

Closes the currently open modal dialog.

=for head2 :numbered
Focus($widget)

Focuses a specific widget. Returns the widget for chaining. Handles
C<.focusable-widget> delegation automatically.

=for head2 :numbered
FocusNext

Moves focus to the next focusable widget.

=for head2 :numbered
FocusPrevious

Moves focus to the previous focusable widget.

=for head2 :numbered
SwitchScreen(Str $name)

Switches to a named screen.

=for head2 :numbered
Quit

Exits the TUI application gracefully.

=for head2 :numbered
Toast(Str $message, Numeric :$duration = 3.0)

Dispatches a transient toast notification via the app.

